---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Authors from '../../components/Authors.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import ScanPipeline from '../../components/figures/ScanPipeline.astro';
import ResearchArt from '../../components/ResearchArt.astro';
import { publications } from '../../data/publications';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
import { personRef } from '../../lib/seo';

const review = publications.find((p) => p.key === 'icccbe2026')!;
const ar = publications.find((p) => p.key === 'i3ce2026')!;
---

<Base
  title="Scan-to-BIM with mobile LiDAR"
  og="scan-to-bim"
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Work', path: 'work/' },
    { name: 'Scan-to-BIM with mobile LiDAR', path: 'work/scan-to-bim/' },
  ]}
  schema={[
    {
      '@type': 'TechArticle',
      headline: 'Scan-to-BIM with mobile LiDAR: how far a quick scan can go toward a usable IFC model',
      author: personRef(Astro.site),
      datePublished: '2026-10-29',
      about: "Master's research on scan-to-BIM with mobile LiDAR at the NTU H-Team lab",
      mainEntityOfPage: new URL(url('work/scan-to-bim/'), Astro.site).href,
      image: new URL(url('og/scan-to-bim.png'), Astro.site).href,
    },
  ]}
  current="work"
  description="A working note on my master's research at NTU: how far a quick, low-cost mobile LiDAR scan can go toward an as-built IFC model an engineer can actually use."
>
  <div class="grid-paper">
    <div class="container page" style="padding-bottom: clamp(32px, 4vw, 56px)">
      <div class="page-head">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a href={url('work/')} class="underline-link" style="color: inherit">Work</a> / Research note</p>
        <h1 class="page-title" data-vt style="--vt: case-scan">Scan-to-BIM with mobile LiDAR</h1>
        <p class="lede" style="max-width: 46rem">
          Can a quick, low-cost scan of an existing building become an IFC model an engineer can actually
          use? That is the question behind my master's research in the H-Team lab. This page is a working
          note: what the pipeline has to do, and what the result has to answer.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="stats">
        <div><dt>Input</dt><dd class="stats-text">Mobile LiDAR</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Output</dt><dd class="stats-text">IFC</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Focus</dt><dd class="stats-text">As-built</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Status</dt><dd class="stats-text">In progress</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <p class="footnote" style="margin-top: 22px">
        Working note, October 2026. The method and the open questions are here; results are not claimed yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="container page" style="padding-top: 24px">
    <section aria-labelledby="question">
      <SectionHead no={1} id="question" title="The question" />
      <div class="case-prose">
        <p>
          Most existing buildings have no model anyone can work from. Producing one means a survey: slow,
          expensive, and usually outsourced. A mobile LiDAR capture is the opposite trade — one
          walk-through, a point cloud in minutes, and no guarantee that what comes out is clean enough to
          model.
        </p>
        <p>
          The research sits between those two. Not "can a scan produce a model", but how much of a model a
          quick scan can support before the cleanup costs more than the survey it replaced.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="why" class="case-section">
      <SectionHead no={2} id="why" title="Why it matters" />
      <div class="case-prose">
        <p>
          Retrofit decisions depend on geometry: where the walls, slabs and openings actually are. My
          ICCCBE 2026 review read scan-to-BIM through that lens — what a team upgrading an older building
          needs from the model, not how dense the point cloud is.
        </p>
        <p>
          The output has to be trustworthy enough to measure, not just impressive to look at. That is a
          higher bar than a good-looking render, and it is the bar the work is aimed at.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="pipeline" class="case-section">
      <SectionHead no={3} id="pipeline" title="The pipeline, in five jobs" />
      <p class="case-lead">
        Every scan-to-BIM pipeline has to do the same five jobs. The research question is how much of each
        one a quick mobile scan can carry.
      </p>
      <div class="case-figure" data-reveal>
        <ScanPipeline />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="answer" class="case-section">
      <SectionHead no={4} id="answer" title="What the result has to answer" />
      <ul class="takeaways">
        <li>
          <strong>Which elements survive the scan.</strong> Walls and slabs are the easy case. Openings,
          thin partitions and services are where a model starts to guess.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How much manual cleanup is left.</strong> A model that takes two days to repair is not
          faster than the survey it replaced.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Whether an engineer can trust it.</strong> The test is not a rendered image. It is
          whether someone can open the IFC, measure it, and work from it.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="hard" class="case-section">
      <SectionHead no={5} id="hard" title="The hard cases" />
      <div class="case-prose">
        <p>
          Some failures are predictable, and any honest evaluation has to include them: glass and
          reflective surfaces that return weak or missing points; furniture and clutter that hide the
          surfaces that matter; ceilings and services a standing scan never sees; thin elements that never
          return a clean face.
        </p>
        <p>
          A pipeline that only works on an empty, orthogonal room is not a result. Those rooms are the
          exception, not the test.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="papers" class="case-section">
      <SectionHead no={6} id="papers" title="The papers" />
      {
        [review, ar].map((p) => (
          <article class="paper" data-inview>
            <div class="paper-thumb">
              <ResearchArt id={p.key} />
            </div>
            <div class="paper-body">
              <h3 class="pub-title">{p.title}</h3>
              <Authors authors={p.authors} />
              <p class="pub-venue">{p.venue ?? p.year}</p>
              <p class="pub-summary">{p.summary}</p>
              <div class="chip-row pub-links">
                {p.links.map((l) => (
                  <a class="link-chip" href={l.href}>
                    {l.label} <span class="arrow arrow--ne" aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
                  </a>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))
      }
      <p style="margin-top: 32px">
        <a href={url('research/')} class="underline-link"><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">←</span> All research</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
